# Hookup Review — the Delivery Gate for the Artifact That Becomes a Joint [SPEC:CXC-HKR]

> A decision-support doc that is misread produces a wrong ANSWER, caught in the next exchange. A wiring diagram that is misread produces a wrong JOINT — executed with a soldering iron, on boards that may already be panelized, surfacing hours later as hardware behaving inexplicably. Until 2026-08-15 the low-cost artifact (the DSD) had a purpose-built interactive path and the high-cost artifact (the hookup guide) had a generic file send. That inversion is the whole reason this endpoint exists, and the reason its contract is a GATE rather than a transport.

---

## 1. The endpoint

### [CXC-HKR-MCP] `send_hookup_for_human_review` is the interactive wiring-diagram delivery path
- definition: the architect composes a maker `.compose.yaml` carrying a `hookup:` block, then calls `send_hookup_for_human_review(hookup_path, warnings?, correlation_id?, mqtt_url?)` on the decision-ops MCP surface. The service validates the scene is a real, repo-scoped, PARSEABLE netlist; renders the guide; gates on the labels having survived rasterization; publishes an `_app/hookup/open` intent carrying the SOURCE scene, both rendered halves and the negative-space warnings; and returns a correlatable status.
- requirement: it lives on the ONE decision-ops surface beside `send_dsd_for_human_review` ([DD-DSDV-MCP-HOME] RESOLVED) — a hookup guide is decision materials, and a second server for a second artifact type is a fork of one delivery contract.
- requirement: it MUST appear on `mcp_server.TOOLS` AND in `DecisionOpsMcp.dispatch`'s handler map; dropping it from EITHER surface is the regression [CXC-DSDV-REGISTER] already named, and `tests/test_decision_ops_hookup.py` guards both.
- constraint: ANY validation failure publishes NOTHING — an unresolvable path, a pre-rendered image, an unsound netlist, an unlabelled render, or a drawing carrying an un-bridged crossing ([CXC-HKR-CROSSING-GATE]).
- relates-to: [CXC-DSDV-MCP], [CXC-DSDV-REGISTER], [CXC-HKR-CROSSING-GATE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HKR]

### [CXC-HKR-RENDER-NOT-ACCEPT] The endpoint takes the SCENE and RENDERS; a pre-rendered image is refused
- premise: a rendered image cannot prove which netlist it came from. A stale PNG is byte-for-byte indistinguishable from a fresh one, and what it produces is a wrong joint discovered hours later.
- requirement: `hookup_path` is the `.compose.yaml`. The SVG and PNG are produced inside the endpoint, from that netlist, at send time. A `.png`/`.svg`/`.jpg`/`.pdf` argument is REFUSED with a message naming what to pass instead.
- rationale: it costs the caller nothing — the scene is single-source for both halves (netlist + bus bindings) and the intent has to carry it anyway. It also buys the strict S2 netlist validation for free (a wire to a pin that does not exist on that part is an ERROR), so a picture of an unsound netlist is never sent. And it is what makes the label gate possible at all: the gate's reference render must come from the SAME rasterizer that produced the delivered image.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HKR]

---

## 2. The label gate — the reason it is a gate and not a transport

### [CXC-HKR-LABEL-GATE] An unlabelled diagram is REFUSED, never merely flagged
- premise: `composeio.maker.hookup_svg.svgdom_rasterize` — the rasterization core of the shipped `hookup_svg_to_png` — silently drops EVERY text label. skia-python's `SVGDOM.MakeFromStream` has no `Builder` overload, so the DOM is built with no font manager: every `<text>` renders to nothing, with no exception, no warning and no missing-glyph box. The PNG is valid, the geometry is perfect, and the part ids, pin names and net labels are gone. An instrument that can only report success.
- requirement: the endpoint REFUSES to publish when the labels did not survive, setting `refused_unlabelled: true`. A diagram with no labels is not a diagram — every wire endpoint's identity lives in the text.
- rationale: REFUSE rather than FLAG, because "a picture with a warning attached to it" is precisely the delivery mode that already failed. The human looks at the picture; a status blob nobody reads is not a gate. There is no legitimate case for sending an unlabelled wiring diagram, so nothing is lost by refusing.
- constraint: the gate also fires at the SOURCE — a rendered guide whose SVG carries zero non-empty `<text>` elements is refused before anything is rasterized.
- relates-to: [CXC-HKR-LABEL-DETECTOR], [CXC-HKR-FAULT-INJECTOR]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HKR]

### [CXC-HKR-LABEL-DETECTOR] The detector is a same-rasterizer differential, not OCR and not a golden image
- definition: rasterize the guide SVG, then rasterize the SAME SVG with every `<text>` element stripped, THROUGH THE SAME RASTERIZER, and compare the two rasters. Identical rasters mean the text contributed exactly zero pixels.
- rationale: renderer-agnostic by construction. No OCR, no font knowledge, no reference image to regenerate, and — because both sides go through whichever rasterizer produced the delivered image — no false verdict from antialiasing differences between two different renderers. It answers the question actually being asked ("did the labels reach THIS image?") rather than a proxy for it.
- constraint: the HARD refusal is on zero label ink, which is the known defect's binary signature. Sparse-but-nonzero ink only ADDS a note to the status: a partial-drop threshold would be arbitrary and would false-refuse a legitimately sparse diagram.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HKR]
- implementation: ONE implementation, N consumers — `composeio.maker.hookup_svg.label_ink_report`, which lives beside the rasterizers it measures. Two consumers wrap it with their own SEMANTICS and neither reimplements the comparison: `hookup_svg.rasterize_with_label_proof` raises and DELETES the file (the renderer cannot produce a mute PNG at all), and `decision_ops.hookup_review.label_survival` returns the report so the endpoint can refuse to PUBLISH. `strip_text` / `count_text_elements` / `SPARSE_LABEL_RATIO` are the same single definitions, lazily re-exported by the endpoint so that module stays importable without the render engine.
- change: 2026-08-15 — COLLAPSED A ONE-TABLE FORK. This detector existed TWICE: `hookup_review.label_survival` (built for the endpoint, commit 7ef4248) and `hookup_svg.rasterize_with_label_proof` (built for the render-defect fix, da8a84e), written hours apart the same day. Kept the SECOND as the primitive — it is renderer-agnostic by construction and deletes the mute output rather than leaving a PNG on disk — and the endpoint now CALLS it. The measurement is bit-identical before and after (dual_throttle perfboard: 78 `<text>`, 0 px through `svgdom_rasterize`, 7,917 px through `canvas_rasterize` at scale 1.0 / 31,237 at 2.0, old and new agreeing exactly at both scales).
- context-for-change: THE INVALIDATING ASSUMPTION was that a detector belongs to the FEATURE that needed it. It belongs to the THING IT MEASURES — the rasterizers — and every feature that needs it is a consumer. This is the THIRD instance of one defect class: `[BP-TYPE-CODES]` collapsed four drifting copies of the message-type table after every copy ended in a `.get(type, 0)` and an ACK silently arrived as a STATE, and the value codec repeated it one layer down. Neither agent here was careless — they could not see each other, and both were right that the check was needed. CONCURRENT DISPATCH MANUFACTURES FORKS UNLESS THE SHARED THING IS NAMED, so the collapse is the fix and the NAMING is the prevention: `label_ink_report` is now a named, exported, docstring-declared shared surface, and `tests/test_decision_ops_hookup.py::test_h4_the_detector_is_NOT_implemented_in_this_module` fails if a future author writes a second compare in the consumer. ALSO FOUND: the brief for this collapse asserted that a "gate disabled -> the mute diagram is PUBLISHED" control existed. It did not — H4 proved the gate FIRES but never that the gate was the only thing in the way, so it could have been deleted with every test still green. That control now exists. An assumed control is worse than an absent one.

### [CXC-HKR-FAULT-INJECTOR] The broken rasterizer is KEPT, public, and named — it is how the gate is tested
- requirement: `hookup_svg.svgdom_rasterize` stays public and stays documented as label-dropping. `tests/test_decision_ops_hookup.py` injects that REAL function as the endpoint's rasterizer and asserts a refusal with nothing published.
- rationale: a gate whose failure mode cannot be reproduced is not a tested gate. Deleting the broken path would make the gate unfalsifiable, and a synthetic stand-in would test a story about the defect rather than the defect. The two rasterizers deliberately share one signature so either is injectable wherever a rasterizer is a parameter.
- constraint: `svgdom_rasterize` MUST NOT be used to produce an artifact a human reads a wiring diagram off; `canvas_rasterize` (which lowers through this repo's own Canvas contract, and therefore its font stack) is the human-facing renderer.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HKR]

### [CXC-HKR-SELFTEST-ROOT] The harness measures the checkout it was LOADED FROM — and a fixture that will not load is a HARNESS failure, never a gate failure
- premise: `decision_ops` resolves repo paths through ONE containment guard whose allowed roots begin with the CANONICAL checkout — correct, and deliberately so, because a permanent RECORD has exactly one home ([CXC-DSDV], `graph_home`). A repo-RELATIVE path therefore binds to the canonical tree FIRST. That is right for an artifact a human named and wrong for a FIXTURE, because a fixture's job is to characterise the code it ships beside.
- requirement: an in-module `_selftest()` MUST make every fixture path ABSOLUTE against `dsd_review.own_checkout_root()` — the root of the checkout the module file itself lives in — before passing it to the endpoint. Never a relative path.
- requirement: the containment guard's allowed roots include the checkout the CALLING CODE was loaded from, alongside the canonical checkout and the caller's CWD worktree. Every root is still a repo root, so containment is unchanged — `/etc/passwd` and `../../etc/passwd` are still refused.
- requirement: a fixture that will not LOAD is reported ONCE as a HARNESS failure naming the checkout root and the scene, and the behaviour checks downstream of it DO NOT RUN. A harness that lets a setup failure fall through and be counted as a failure of the behaviour under test cannot tell "the gate did not fire" from "the gate never ran".
- rationale: the two failure modes have opposite urgencies and the harness was reporting the benign one in the words of the catastrophic one. A false RED on the label gate says *unlabelled wiring diagrams are publishable right now*; that is a page-the-architect claim, and it cost a full investigation. The false GREEN is the more dangerous half and the reason this is a requirement rather than a nicety: a broken change in a worktree, validated against known-good CANONICAL fixtures, reports 17/17 and lands.
- constraint: an instrument that reads its inputs from a different tree than the code under test is not measuring that code. This is the `[INSIGHT-STALE-CODE-IS-A-FRESHNESS-CLASS]` shape one level out — there the stale thing was a long-running process's code snapshot, here it is a sibling checkout's data — and the general form is: NAME THE TREE YOU ARE MEASURING.
- recurrence: this is INSTANCE 7 of `[INSIGHT-WORKTREE-IS-NOT-THE-ENVIRONMENT]`, whose own generalization predicted it in full — *every isolation mechanism isolates exactly one axis, and the axes it does not isolate resolve to the shared environment SILENTLY, producing plausible-looking wrong output rather than an error*. Instance 3 of that node was the pre-commit hook deriving its root from `BASH_SOURCE` and therefore gating a worktree commit with MAIN's tests (closed 2026-08-10, `061503b`, by taking the root from the COMMIT CONTEXT). This is the same defect one layer in: `_selftest` took its fixture root from the CANONICAL checkout rather than from the CODE CONTEXT. The hook was fixed; its sibling was never audited. That node ALSO already records "two agents independently misdiagnosed these as pre-existing failures on HEAD because the verification SHARED the blind spot and certified the wrong conclusion" — written 2026-08-06, and re-lived verbatim on 2026-08-15. A recorded class only pays if the next instance is checked AGAINST it, so the durable rule is the one that generalises: WHEN CODE NEEDS TO KNOW A ROOT, ASK WHICH QUESTION IT IS ASKING — "where does the record live?" is the canonical checkout, "what am I made of?" is `own_checkout_root()`, and they are never interchangeable.
- change: 2026-08-15 — `tests/test_decision_ops_hookup.py::test_selftest_is_green` was RED on `origin/main` (`be1ac1f`), reporting checks (9), (10) and (16) failing, and (16) is the label gate. THE GATE WAS INTACT: all twenty other tests in that file — including the fault injection, the paired positive, the detector-in-isolation, the fork tripwire and the anti-vacuity control — passed, and a direct negative control refused the mute rasterization (78 `<text>`, 0 ink px, `refused_unlabelled: true`, nothing published) while passing the good one (31,237 ink px). Only the HARNESS was broken. `_selftest` passed relative fixture paths, so a worktree at `origin/main` rendered the CANONICAL checkout's `dual_throttle.compose.yaml` — and that checkout was parked on a branch predating `ffa232d`, whose wire carried no `subscribes:` seam, so the scene did not parse at all under `origin/main`'s code. All three "failures" were one scene that never loaded; check (16)'s `refused_unlabelled` was absent because the gate never saw a diagram. Check (17) "an unlabelled diagram publishes NOTHING" PASSED throughout — VACUOUSLY, on a send that failed for an unrelated reason, which is the same vacuity hazard [CXC-HKR-LABEL-DETECTOR] already had to name once.
- context-for-change: THE INVALIDATING ASSUMPTION was that "run it on a pristine checkout" isolates a test from the machine. It does not, when the code under test resolves paths through a guard that deliberately reaches OUT of the current checkout. Two agents independently confirmed the failure "pre-existing on pristine `origin/main`" — one by stashing and re-running, one by diffing failure sets against a pristine detached worktree — and got identical results because BOTH were reading the same canonical tree. That was one method run twice wearing two coats, and its agreement was mistaken for independence. The general lesson: two methods only corroborate if they differ in the variable under suspicion. This defect is also invisible to CI, where the canonical checkout IS the checkout under test — it can only fire on a developer machine with a second checkout on another branch, which is precisely the configuration concurrent agent dispatch creates all day.
- relates-to: [CXC-HKR-FAULT-INJECTOR], [CXC-HKR-LABEL-GATE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HKR]

---

## 2b. The crossing gate — the second thing the drawing can lie about

### [CXC-HKR-CROSSING-GATE] A crossing the router could NOT bridge is a HARD FAILURE, not a warning
- premise: where two conductors cross, the horizontal run is drawn with a small bridge so the crossing cannot be read as a junction — junctions are FILLED pads, bridges are unfilled arcs, and the two are disjoint by construction ([SPEC:CXC-SVG] renderer, `hookup_layout.plan_hops`). Some crossings CANNOT be bridged: the bridge would land on a solder pad (`pad`), would swallow the run's own 90-degree corner (`corner`), or would merge into an unreadable blob with a neighbouring bridge (`crowded`). `plan_hops` returns those as a SECOND value, and `SkippedHop`'s own docstring names the stake — silence "would let an un-hopped crossing (which reads as a junction) pass unnoticed", "the same class of lie as a wire that isn't there".
- premise: THE SECOND VALUE WAS DISCARDED AT THE ONLY CALL SITE. `hookup_svg.hookup_to_svg` read `planned, _skipped = plan_hops(lay, segs)` and dropped it, so the mechanism built to prevent the silence WAS the silence. Measured 2026-08-15 on `python/composeio/render/examples/tm1637_quad_panel.compose.yaml`: 57 hops planned, **7 skipped** (6 `crowded`, 1 `corner`), five of them piled at ONE congested column at x=360. Seven perfboard intersections that read as solder joints, on a guide that was open on glass.
- requirement: `send_hookup_for_human_review` REFUSES when any crossing is unbridged — publishes NOTHING, returns `status: error` with `refused_unhopped_crossing: true`. RULING (David, 2026-08-15, verbatim): *"let's start with skipped crossing being a hard failure, and if needed we can relax."*
- requirement: THE REFUSAL IS A WORK ORDER, NOT A COUNT. `"7 crossings skipped"` is a dead end — the author has to re-route a SPECIFIC column. Every skipped crossing is named by BOTH conductors (`part.pin -> part.pin`), its coordinate, and its reason; and each distinct reason carries its remedy (`hookup_layout.skip_remedy`). A refusal a reader cannot act on gets worked around, not fixed.
- constraint: the gate runs BEFORE rasterization, because it is a property of the DRAWING and because no raster check can substitute for it — a crossing the planner skipped has no arc in the document, so [CXC-HKR-HOP-SURVIVAL] is blind to it BY CONSTRUCTION. Two failures, two instruments; conflating them is how the first one survived.
- constraint: `hookup_svg_to_png` and `hookup_svg_to_file` do NOT refuse on skips. They are the artifact-BUILD path, and a build that cannot produce the picture cannot show an author what to re-route. The refusal belongs at DELIVERY, where a human is about to solder.
- consequence: `tm1637_quad_panel.compose.yaml` is UNDELIVERABLE through this endpoint until its layout is re-routed. That is the ruling working, not a regression, and it is stated here because a gate whose first victim is a surprise gets relaxed for the wrong reason.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HKR]
- implementation: `hookup_svg.render_hookup(hookup) -> (svg, CrossingReport)` is the FULL render result and the only entry point a delivery path may use; `hookup_to_svg` is its first element and is kept, byte-identical, for callers that only want pixels (verified: both committed scenes hash-identical to `origin/main` before and after). `hookup_layout.CrossingReport` carries `hops`, `skipped`, `wires`, and `.ok` / `.skipped_rows()` / `.as_dict()` / `.skipped_text()`. The endpoint's render seam is `hookup_review.render_guide`; the old `hookup_review.hookup_to_svg` indirection was REMOVED rather than kept, so a stale `monkeypatch.setattr(hr, "hookup_to_svg", ...)` raises AttributeError instead of silently patching something nothing calls — a fault injector that no longer injects is the loudest possible false green.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HKR]

### [CXC-HKR-HOP-SURVIVAL] The bridges must reach the PIXELS — the label gate's missing sibling
- premise: the label gate exists because a rasterizer can silently drop every `<text>` and return a valid PNG. NOBODY BUILT THE EQUIVALENT FOR THE ARCS. Verified 2026-08-15: all 57 of the TM1637 panel's hops DO reach the SVG (57 arc commands across 18 conductor paths, radius 7.5) — so a delivered PNG missing them loses them DOWNSTREAM, invisibly. A label-stripped render was refused; an arc-stripped one sailed through the identical gate, and it is the worse artifact of the two because it still looks complete.
- requirement: the endpoint REFUSES a render whose line-hop arcs contributed zero pixels, sets `refused_unhopped_crossing: true`, and the bridge-free PNG is DELETED, not delivered — the same rule and the same reason as the mute raster.
- definition: the detector is [CXC-HKR-LABEL-DETECTOR]'s differential with ONE substitution — `strip_hops` in place of `strip_text`. Each arc is replaced by a straight `L` TO ITS OWN ENDPOINT, so the conductor stays a valid, identically-terminated polyline and the only difference from the delivered document is the bridge. (Deleting the command instead would break the path and make the differential measure the BREAKAGE; it happens to be harmless today only because every hop is collinear with its run, which is one routing change from untrue — pinned by test.)
- constraint: THE ARC SCAN IS SCOPED TO `data-wire` CONDUCTORS. Footprint art contributes `<path>` elements of its own, and a document-wide strip would let ART pixels satisfy the differential — a check that can pass because the artwork moved is a check that can only report success. A naive document-wide scan also over-counts: 69 against 57 real bridges on the TM1637 panel, the surplus being pin NAMES (`r1.A`).
- constraint: TWO DELIBERATE ASYMMETRIES WITH THE LABEL GATE, both documented on the report. (1) ZERO ARCS IS LEGITIMATE — a diagram whose conductors never cross declares no bridges and loses none, so `ok` is True; zero `<text>` is NOT legitimate. (2) SPARSE IS PER-BRIDGE — fewer changed pixels than declared bridges means bridges are individually vanishing, a floor that needs no arbitrary constant.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HKR]
- implementation: ONE differential, TWO features — `hookup_svg._ink_vs_reference` is the single comparison, and `label_ink_report` / `hop_ink_report` are two features over it. This is [CXC-HKR-LABEL-DETECTOR]'s collapse honoured PROSPECTIVELY rather than repaired retrospectively: the second check was written against the named shared surface instead of beside it. Consumers: `rasterize_with_hop_proof` (renderer refuses to WRITE), `hookup_review.hop_survival` (endpoint refuses to PUBLISH), and `hookup_svg_to_png` now runs both proofs over ONE raster of the document.

### [CXC-HKR-HOP-FAULT-INJECTOR] `hopless_rasterize` — the negative control the hop gate would otherwise lack
- premise: the label gate has a REAL broken renderer to falsify itself against ([CXC-HKR-FAULT-INJECTOR]). The hop gate has no known one, and a gate whose failure mode cannot be reproduced is not a tested gate.
- requirement: `hookup_svg.hopless_rasterize` is public and documented — it renders the document with every bridge flattened, through the honest `canvas_rasterize`, synthesising exactly the loss: a valid, complete-looking PNG in which every crossing has become a plain intersection. It MUST NEVER produce a delivered artifact; it is a lie by construction.
- payoff: it falsifies BOTH gates in one fixture. The same injected render PASSES the label gate cleanly (its text is perfect) and is REFUSED by the hop gate — which is the whole argument for the second gate existing, asserted rather than narrated.
- constraint: the paired POSITIVE control is not synthetic. `tm1637_quad_panel.compose.yaml` skips 7 crossings TODAY and must be refused; `dual_throttle.compose.yaml` skips 0 and must still DELIVER. Both directions, or the gate is unproven. A test asserts the fixtures are still what they claim, so a future re-route turns THAT test red first instead of quietly hollowing out every gate test beneath it.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HKR]

---

## 2c. The legibility gate — presence is not readability

### [CXC-HKR-LEGIBILITY-GATE] A drawing in which two things are printed on each other is REFUSED, and `label_survival` structurally cannot see it
- premise: the gates above prove a label made PIXELS. They cannot prove it can be READ, and the difference shipped TWICE. Delivery 1 (2026-08-15): the R503 + ESP32-S3 guide passed everything — 67 crossings bridged / 0 skipped; 178 labels, 59153 ink px, `ok: true`; 67 bridges, 19103 ink px, `ok: true`; plus a measured "0 body overlaps". David: *"the fingerprint sensor placement is so close to the pins that the wiring is unreadable."* 178 of 178 labels present, and colliding.
- premise: THE INSTRUMENT WAS NOT WRONG, IT WAS ASKED THE WRONG QUESTION. [CXC-HKR-LABEL-DETECTOR] is a differential against a text-STRIPPED render, so it answers "did any text reach the pixels" — and 178 labels stamped on ONE SPOT answer it YES. Its answer was read as the other question's. Meanwhile the body check answered "no overlap" TRUTHFULLY about two parts at EXACTLY 0.0 clearance: a strict interior-intersection test asked a CLEARANCE question. The clearance rule already existed (`hookup_layout._NESTLE_MARGIN`) and was simply never applied to `at:`-anchored parts; it is now `PART_CLEARANCE`.
- requirement: `send_hookup_for_human_review` REFUSES when any collision class has a row — `status: error`, `refused_illegible: true`, publishes NOTHING. Pre-raster, for the same reason as [CXC-HKR-CROSSING-GATE]: two labels on one point still make ink, so no differential can see it.
- definition: FOUR HARD CLASSES, measured on the EMITTED DOCUMENT through the engine's own `set_font`/`measure_text` — the same path `canvas_rasterize` draws with, so the gate cannot drift from the renderer by construction. `label_label` (two labels overprinting); `label_body` (a label across a part it does not belong to — a part's OWN labels on its OWN body are deliberate and exempt, attributed by the `data-owner` the renderer emits); `body_body` (two parts closer than `PART_CLEARANCE`, reported as CLEARANCE so the tangent case is a failure); and `body_wire` ([CXC-HKR-BODY-WIRE]).
- constraint: `label_wire` IS A NOTE AND MUST STAY ONE. On a 26-wire board a label crossing SOME conductor is unavoidable, and a gate with an unreachable bar gets relaxed until it means nothing. It is REPORTED because labels were once drawn UNDER the wires and the MCU's whole top row vanished under the ground rail with every signal green — the z-order fix is what makes an overlap merely dense rather than a deletion, and the count is how a human sees how dense.
- constraint: A PASS IS A PRECONDITION FOR ASKING DAVID, NEVER A SUBSTITUTE. Zero collisions means no two things are printed on each other; density, contrast and colour live outside it. `[PRINCIPLE-MACHINE-GREEN-IS-NOT-GLASS-GREEN]` — this gate can only ever refuse.
- relates-to: [CXC-HKR-BODY-WIRE], [CXC-HKR-LABEL-GATE], [CXC-HKR-CROSSING-GATE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HKR]
- implementation: `maker.hookup_svg.legibility_report(svg, layout, *, measure, segs)`; endpoint adapter `hookup_review.legibility_survival`; refusal `hookup_review.legibility_refusal`, a WORK ORDER carrying coordinates the way `crossing_refusal` does. The gate's OWN geometry is pinned against ACTUAL INK (`test_rotated_label_box_matches_the_ink`) because the first draft had a sign backwards and measured every downward label 24 px above where it draws — which no amount of re-reading the algebra finds.

### [CXC-HKR-BODY-WIRE] A conductor drawn beneath a part body is a HARD failure — the un-hopped crossing, one layer up
- premise: delivery 2 (2026-08-16) passed the gate above — 117 crossings all bridged, 0 skipped, 0 collisions, `ok: true`. David: *"I COULD use this diagram, but it's really not quite right — see how the FP component and its wires overlay the gnd for tm2 and tm4."* Measured: `mcu.GND -> tm2.GND` and `mcu.GND -> tm4.GND` (and both 5 V runs) rode ONE lane at y=858.0 for the FULL 134.2 px width of the R503, 4.4 px above its six pads, through its pin band and its captions.
- premise: THIS IS A DIFFERENT CLASS FROM A LABEL GRAZING A WIRE. `plan_hops` bridges wire × wire so a crossing READS as a crossing. A conductor passing under an OPAQUE part body gets no such treatment — it cannot be hopped and it cannot be followed, and the reader loses the conductor for the width of the part. On a wiring guide, *"where does this ground go"* is precisely the question the drawing exists to answer.
- requirement: HARD, and kept strictly apart from the `label_wire` note. The soft bucket was NOT widened to absorb it and MUST NOT BE — the soft bucket is soft because a hard bar there is unreachable, and THIS bar is reachable (the same scene passes it today, measured).
- definition: for each part P and each conductor W with NEITHER endpoint on P, the length W runs inside `hookup_layout.part_extent(P)` grown by `CONDUCTOR_HALF_W` (half the drawn 4.8 px stroke). ONE constant, two callers — the `label_wire` note and this refusal both ask "does the conductor AS DRAWN share space with this", and it is also what makes the tangent case a failure: a trunk grazing a part's extent still prints 2.6 px of copper on it.
- constraint: THE OWN-PART EXEMPTION IS TOTAL, DELIBERATELY, AND IT IS NAMED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. A wire that TERMINATES on a part is exempt from that part — the reader does not lose it there, it ENDS there, and a lead must reach its own pad through its own pin band. What stops a lead raking across its OWN part is a different rule that already holds: `_polyline_body_clear`, bounded by `_exit_budget`. The slab is covered there, the band is covered here, neither twice.
- consequence: THE ROUTER WAS TAUGHT THE SAME RECTANGLE, because a gate that can only be obeyed and never satisfied gets relaxed. `hookup_layout._assign_routes` routes against `_part_extent`, not `body` — a GENERATED horizontal part draws its slab ABOVE its pin row, so the pads, the stubs and every caption sat OUTSIDE the obstacle rectangle. The router was truthful about the slab and blind to the band, the same shape as the tangent-body defect before it: not a wrong comparison, a comparison against the wrong rectangle. Measured: R503 panel 117 → 118 crossings, 0 skipped either way, 4 of 5 rows gone; `dual_throttle` and the C3 panel unchanged.
- payoff: THE INSTRUMENT FOUND A DEFECT NOBODY REPORTED, which is the whole reason to build one. `r3` and `r4` shared column 28, so `r3.A` and `r4.A` shared an x and the shared-CLK trunk climbing to the TOP resistor was drawn STRAIGHT THROUGH the bottom one — 43.7 px of conductor across a 46.2 px part, over its `A` pad and under its own name. No lane cleared, so the router took its documented least-bad route and drew the wire over the part. `r4` moved to column 27.
- constraint: the negative control is PINNED AS DATA, not re-rendered. The router fix means re-rendering the scene no longer reproduces what David was sent, and a control a later fix can silently disarm is not a control — so the two ground trunks are recorded verbatim as routed on `1c7b4d2` (`test_the_delivered_ground_trunks_are_refused`).
- relates-to: [CXC-HKR-LEGIBILITY-GATE], [CXC-HKR-CROSSING-GATE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HKR]
- implementation: `maker.hookup_svg.conductor_body_runs(layout, segs)` returns one row per (wire, part) with the run length, the entry coordinate and the part width, so the refusal is a work order. Geometry is `hookup_layout._seg_rect_overlap` — the router's own predicate, not a second copy that can disagree with it. Selftest checks (27)/(28); 25 compliance tests in `tests/render/test_hookup_legibility_gate.py`.

### [CXC-HKR-CAPTION-BESIDE-ITS-LEAD] A turned pin caption stands BESIDE the lead leaving its pad, and the backing plate is REFUSED
- premise: `_ROT_NUDGE` centres a turned caption's glyph column on the pad's x — which is EXACTLY the x that pad's lead escapes along. Every turned caption on a part whose leads leave perpendicular to its pin row therefore printed on its own conductor. Measured: 9 of 9 in `dual_throttle`, 23 of 29 on the R503 panel — and the near-black `GND` caption sat on the near-black ground conductor and was, in the raster, GONE. On the one part in this fleet whose headline hazard is a reversed VCC/GND, that is a safety surface, not typography.
- requirement: the caption steps aside by half a glyph column plus half a conductor plus a pixel of air (`_rot_lead_clear`), always in the SAME direction for the whole drawing — a builder should never have to work out which side of a pad a name belongs to, the same reason every turned label reads bottom-to-top.
- constraint: THE BACKING PLATE IS REFUSED, and the reason is [CXC-HKR-BODY-WIRE] itself. A plate restores contrast by BREAKING THE CONDUCTOR beneath it — a reader losing a conductor for the width of an opaque thing, which is the class immediately above. A smaller copy of the same lie is not a fix. (A blanket plate on every label in every scene was also refused, twice, on scope: it is a whole-fleet visual change nobody asked for.)
- payoff: measured after the offset — R503 turned-captions-on-ink 23 → 9, all-labels-on-ink 43 → 28; `dual_throttle` 9 → 4 and 23 → 18. No new collisions, no new skipped crossings.
- constraint: the em figure (`_ROT_HALF_COL_EM = 0.74`, from a measured ascent 8.0 + descent 3.0 at 7.5 px) carries the same contract as `_ADVANCE_EM` — an over-estimate merely leaves air, and [CXC-HKR-LEGIBILITY-GATE] measures the REAL boxes either way. The heuristic decides; the gate is the truth.
- relates-to: [CXC-HKR-BODY-WIRE], [CXC-HKR-LEGIBILITY-GATE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HKR]

---

## 3. Negative space — what the drawing cannot draw

### [CXC-HKR-NEGATIVE-SPACE] The connections that must NOT exist ride the intent
- premise: a netlist can only express connections that EXIST. The load-bearing knowledge in a hookup guide is frequently about connections that must NOT — "do not butt the modules together, it commons DIO"; "leave the `-B` header unconnected"; "do not add pull-ups, the module has them"; "never use GPIO2/8/9". A diagram cannot draw an absence.
- requirement: the intent carries `warnings: [{text, kind, about, source}]` and the viewer renders them as a DO-NOT panel BESIDE the drawing — in the one place the human is already looking. A guide whose warnings live in a separate markdown file is a guide whose warnings are not read.
- definition: two sources today — the caller's `warnings=` argument (`source: caller`) and a tolerantly-read `hookup: do_not:` block in the scene (`source: scene`).
- constraint: a scene with NO negative space is NOTED in the returned status, never refused. Almost every hookup guide has at least one "do not", so zero is suspicious — but a hard refusal would be a rule about authorship, not about correctness.
- relates-to: [CXC-HKR-DO-NOT-SCHEMA]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HKR]

### [CXC-HKR-DO-NOT-SCHEMA] PROPOSED — `hookup: do_not:` as a first-class, CHECKABLE netlist section
- status: proposed — awaiting ratify (read tolerantly from raw YAML today; deliberately NOT added to the strict S2 parser by the endpoint build)
- definition: a `do_not:` list beside `wires:`, each entry `{text, kind, about}` where `kind` is one of `no-common | leave-unconnected | no-pullup | reserved-pin | no-power-order | note` and `about` names real part/pin ids (`disp0`, `mcu.GPIO2`). A bare string is accepted as `{text, kind: note}`.
- rationale: it belongs in the SCENE for the same single-source reason the netlist does — a warning that lives in the guide markdown drifts from the drawing the moment either is edited; one that lives beside `wires:` cannot.
- payoff: `about:` is what makes the negative space CHECKABLE rather than decorative. A `reserved-pin` entry naming `mcu.GPIO2` can be cross-checked against the netlist and become a HARD ERROR when a wire actually lands there; `no-common` can be checked against the routed topology. The viewer can also HIGHLIGHT the referenced pins in the drawing — which is how an absence finally gets a visual anchor without drawing a wire that does not exist.
- invalidating-assumption: that authors will write `about:` at all. If the field is left empty in practice the cross-checks never fire and this degrades to prose in a different file — measure adoption before building the checker.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HKR]

---

## 4. The consumer

### [CXC-HKR-LAUNCHER] The `_app/hookup/open` consumer lands in the SAME change as the endpoint
- requirement: `intelligence/tools/hookup_viewer_launcher.py` subscribes `_app/hookup/open` and spawns one guide window per scene, and it is REGISTERED in `intelligence/console/data_sources.py`.
- rationale: an intent nobody subscribes is an intent published into the void — the DSD notify leg already shipped exactly that defect, publishing to `_app/dsd/notice`, a namespace no party consumed. Built-tested-never-registered is a named class here ([DEC-BUILT-TESTED-NEVER-REGISTERED-IS-A-CLASS]), so the endpoint and its consumer are one change.
- definition: the window hosts the NATIVE `hookup` widget pointed at the SOURCE `.compose.yaml`, not the rasterized PNG — so the human gets a resizable, fit-to-pane guide with screen-sized labels, and the picture on screen cannot disagree with the netlist because it IS the netlist. The rendered `.svg`/`.png` still ride the intent for a caller that wants to attach them elsewhere.
- constraint: the intent leg uses `subscribe_pattern`, not an exact subscribe — re-sending the SAME hookup after a correction is the normal case, and `Namespace.publish_state` notifies exact subscribers only when the value CHANGED ([CXC-SOB-SUBSCRIBE]).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HKR]

